{"id":"neet-2026-q137","pn":137,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Sickle cell anaemia","q":"The sixth mutant codon of beta globin gene causing polymerization of Haemoglobin and change in RBC shape is ____ .","o":["CAG","GUG","AUG","GAG"],"c":1,"s":"Sickle cell anaemia is an autosomal recessive disorder.\nIt is caused by substitution of glutamic acid by valine at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of haemoglobin.\nThis amino acid change results from a single base substitution, GAG to GUG.\nHence GUG is responsible for the change in the shape of RBC."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q144","pn":144,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"ABO blood group inheritance","q":"What is the probability of having children with 'O' blood group, where both mother and father are heterozygous for 'A' and 'B' blood group, respectively?","o":["50%","0%","75%","25%"],"c":3,"s":"Parents are Iᴬi and Iᴮi (i recessive): Iᴬi × Iᴮi.\nGametes: Iᴬ, i from one parent and Iᴮ, i from the other.\nOffspring: IᴬIᴮ (AB), Iᴬi (A), Iᴮi (B) and ii (O).\nOut of four possible children, one has blood group O.\nSo the probability of O blood group is 25%.","sfig":{"w":290,"h":156,"alt":"Cross Iᴬi × Iᴮi: gametes Iᴬ, i and Iᴮ, i give IᴬIᴮ (AB), Iᴬi (A), Iᴮi (B), ii (O).","d":[["t",4,18,"Parent :","s"],["t",100,18,"Iᴬi",""],["t",160,18,"×",""],["t",200,18,"Iᴮi",""],["l",160,24,160,40,"a"],["t",4,60,"gametes :","s"],["c",105,56,12,""],["t",105,60,"Iᴬ","m"],["c",148,56,12,""],["t",148,60,"i","m"],["c",206,56,12,""],["t",206,60,"Iᴮ","m"],["c",250,56,12,""],["t",250,60,"i","m"],["t",4,128,"F₁","s"],["t",105,128,"IᴬIᴮ","m"],["t",150,128,"Iᴬi","m"],["t",208,128,"Iᴮi","m"],["t",252,128,"ii","m"],["l",105,68,105,114,""],["l",206,68,105,114,""],["l",105,68,150,114,""],["l",250,68,150,114,""],["l",148,68,208,114,""],["l",206,68,208,114,""],["l",148,68,252,114,""],["l",250,68,252,114,""],["t",105,146,"(AB)","ms"],["t",150,146,"(A)","ms"],["t",208,146,"(B)","ms"],["t",252,146,"(O)","ms"],["t",4,146,"Blood group :","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q145","pn":145,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"ABO blood group inheritance","q":"For a person with blood group ‘O’, which of the following is not a possible combination of parents’ blood group genotypes?","o":["Father : IAi and Mother : IAi","Father : IBi and Mother : IBi","Father : IAIB and Mother : IAi","Father : IAi and Mother : IBi"],"c":2,"s":"When IA and IB are present together, both express their own type of sugars.\nSo an IAIB parent can never give a child of blood group O.\nFor a child to have type O blood, both parents must carry the recessive ‘O’ allele (i) and pass it down.","sfig":{"w":290,"h":190,"alt":"Cross: male IᴬIᴮ × female Iᴬi; lines from the gametes give IᴬIᴬ (‘A’ blood group), Iᴬi (‘A’ blood group), IᴬIᴮ (‘AB’ blood group) and Iᴮi (‘B’ blood group).","d":[["t",20,22,"♂","m"],["t",45,22,"Iᴬ","m"],["t",80,22,"Iᴮ","m"],["t",160,22,"×","m"],["t",215,22,"Iᴬ","m"],["t",245,22,"i","m"],["t",275,22,"♀","m"],["l",45,28,55,125,""],["l",45,28,120,125,""],["l",80,28,185,125,""],["l",80,28,250,125,""],["l",215,28,55,125,""],["l",215,28,185,125,""],["l",245,28,120,125,""],["l",245,28,250,125,""],["t",55,142,"IᴬIᴬ","m"],["t",55,158,"(‘A’ blood","ms"],["t",55,170,"group)","ms"],["t",120,142,"Iᴬi","m"],["t",120,158,"(‘A’ blood","ms"],["t",120,170,"group)","ms"],["t",185,142,"IᴬIᴮ","m"],["t",185,158,"(‘AB’","ms"],["t",185,170,"blood","ms"],["t",185,182,"group)","ms"],["t",250,142,"Iᴮi","m"],["t",250,158,"(‘B’ blood","ms"],["t",250,170,"group)","ms"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026-q154","pn":154,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Sex determination XO type","q":"In a population of a grasshopper species, the chromosome number of some members is 23 and some other members possess 24 chromosomes. The 23 and 24 chromosome-bearing members in this species are ____ .","o":["all males","all females","females and males, respectively","males and females, respectively"],"c":3,"s":"Grasshopper sex determination is XX–XO type.\nMales have only one X chromosome besides the autosomes (XO). Females have a pair of X chromosomes besides the autosomes (XX).\nSo the individual with 23 chromosomes is a male and the one with 24 chromosomes is a female."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q173","pn":173,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Chromosomal disorders","q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Down's syndrome is caused by the absence of one of the X-chromosomes.\nStatement II: Turner's syndrome is caused by the presence of an additional copy of the chromosomes.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"c":0,"s":"Down's syndrome is caused by an additional copy of chromosome 21 (trisomy 21). So Statement I is incorrect.\nTurner's syndrome is caused by absence of one X-chromosome, i.e. 45 with XO. So Statement II is incorrect."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q145","pn":145,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Dihybrid cross","q":"Genes R and Y follow independent assortment. If RRYY produce round yellow seeds and rryy produce wrinkled green seeds, what will be the phenotypic ratio of the F2 generation?","o":["Phenotypic ratio - 1 : 2 : 1","Phenotypic ratio - 3 : 1","Phenotypic ratio - 9 : 3 : 3 : 1","Phenotypic ratio - 9 : 7"],"c":2,"s":"This is a classical dihybrid cross of Mendel.\nA pure round yellow pea plant (RRYY) is crossed with a wrinkled green plant (rryy).\nYellow is dominant over green and round shape is dominant over wrinkled shape.\nPhenotypic ratio in F2 is 9 : 3 : 3 : 1.\nThese are round yellow, round green, wrinkled yellow and wrinkled green.","sfig":{"w":260,"h":90,"alt":"The ratio 9 : 3 : 3 : 1 with arrows to round yellow, round green, wrinkled yellow and wrinkled green.","d":[["t",130,14,"9 : 3 : 3 : 1","m"],["l",110,20,40,48,"a"],["l",122,20,100,48,"a"],["l",138,20,162,48,"a"],["l",150,20,226,48,"a"],["t",35,62,"Round","ms"],["t",35,74,"yellow","ms"],["t",98,62,"Round","ms"],["t",98,74,"green","ms"],["t",162,62,"Wrinkled","ms"],["t",162,74,"yellow","ms"],["t",226,62,"Wrinkled","ms"],["t",226,74,"green","ms"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2025-q152","pn":152,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Pedigree analysis, sex-linked","q":"With the help of given pedigree, find out the probability for the birth of a child having no disease and being a carrier (has the disease mutation in one allele of the gene) in F₃ generation.","o":["1/4","1/2","1/8","Zero"],"c":0,"s":"In F₁ a carrier female and an unaffected (normal, not carrier) male had an affected male child, so the disorder is sex-linked recessive.\nThe consanguineous mating is between female (XᶜX) and male (XᶜY).\nPunnett square gives XᶜXᶜ, XᶜY, XᶜX, XY.\nOut of 4 children only one is a carrier, i.e. 1/4.","fig":{"w":250,"h":308,"alt":"Pedigree of four generations F0 to F3 with a legend; the F3 children are marked with question marks.","d":[["r",99.7,12.3,18.7,18.7,""],["c",164,21.7,10,"F"],["r",36.7,56.7,18.7,18.7,""],["c",89,67.3,10,""],["c",89,67.3,3.5,"F"],["c",191.7,67.3,10,""],["c",191.7,67.3,3.5,"F"],["r",215.7,56.7,18.7,18.7,""],["c",47.3,115.7,10,""],["c",47.3,115.7,3.5,"F"],["c",94.3,115.7,10,""],["c",94.3,115.7,3.5,"F"],["r",154,104,18.7,18.7,"F"],["c",237.7,113.3,10,""],["r",53,155.7,18.7,18.7,""],["c",116.7,165,10,""],["c",168.3,165,10,""],["r",202.3,155.7,18.7,18.7,""],["t",62.3,169.7,"?","ms"],["t",116.7,169.7,"?","ms"],["t",168.3,169.7,"?","ms"],["t",211.7,169.7,"?","ms"],["l",119,21.7,154.3,21.7],["l",140,21.7,140,46.7],["l",89,46.7,191.7,46.7],["l",89,46.7,89,58.3],["l",191.7,46.7,191.7,58.3],["l",56,66,79,66],["l",66.7,66,66.7,93.3],["l",47.3,93.3,94.3,93.3],["l",47.3,93.3,47.3,105.7],["l",94.3,93.3,94.3,105.7],["l",201.7,67.3,215,67.3],["l",207.3,67.3,207.3,91.7],["l",163.3,91.7,237.7,91.7],["l",163.3,91.7,163.3,103.3],["l",237.7,91.7,237.7,103.3],["l",104.3,112,153.3,112],["l",104.3,115.3,153.3,115.3],["l",128.3,115.3,128.3,144.3],["l",60,144.3,210,144.3],["l",62.3,144.3,62.3,155.7],["l",116.7,144.3,116.7,155],["l",168.3,144.3,168.3,155],["l",211.7,144.3,211.7,155.7],["t",80,26.7,"F₀","s"],["t",12.7,70.7,"F₁","s"],["t",12.7,120,"F₂","s"],["t",12.7,169.3,"F₃","s"],["r",22.7,189,18.7,18.7,""],["t",49,202.3,"Unaffected male","s"],["r",22.7,213.7,18.7,18.7,"F"],["t",49,227,"Affected male","s"],["c",32,247.7,10,""],["c",32,247.7,3.5,"F"],["t",49,251.7,"Carrier female","s"],["c",32,272.3,10,""],["t",49,276.3,"Unaffected female","s"],["c",32,297,10,"F"],["t",49,301,"Affected female","s"]]},"sfig":{"w":250,"h":110,"alt":"Punnett square of female XcX with male XcY giving XcXc, XcY, XcX and XY.","d":[["l",100,10,100,100],["l",40,40,235,40],["l",48,16,98,40],["t",44,24,"♀","s"],["t",78,18,"♂","s"],["t",128,32,"Xᶜ","m"],["t",196,32,"Y","m"],["t",62,66,"Xᶜ","m"],["t",62,96,"X","m"],["t",128,66,"XᶜXᶜ","m"],["t",196,66,"XᶜY","m"],["t",128,96,"XᶜX","m"],["t",196,96,"XY","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q185","pn":185,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"Genetic disorders","q":"Match List I with List II:\nA. Down's syndrome — I. 11th chromosome\nB. α-Thalassemia — II. 'X' chromosome\nC. β-Thalassemia — III. 21st chromosome\nD. Klinefelter's syndrome — IV. 16th chromosome","o":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"c":0,"s":"Down's syndrome is due to an extra copy of chromosome 21.\nKlinefelter's syndrome is due to an extra X chromosome.\nα-Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes on chromosome 16 of each parent.\nβ-Thalassemia is controlled by a single gene HBB on chromosome 11 of each parent.\nSo A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q197","pn":197,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Principles of Inheritance and Variation","st":"ABO blood groups","q":"As per ABO blood grouping system, the blood group of father is B⁺, mother is A⁺ and child is O⁺. Their respective genotype can be\nA. Iᴮi / Iᴬi / ii\nB. IᴮIᴮ / IᴬIᴬ / ii\nC. IᴬIᴮ / iIᴬ / Iᴮi\nD. Iᴬi / Iᴮi / Iᴬi\nE. iIᴮ / iIᴬ / IᴬIᴮ\nChoose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["C & B only","D & E only","A only","B only"],"c":2,"s":"Father with blood group B⁺ has genotype Iᴮi (or iIᴮ).\nMother with blood group A⁺ has genotype Iᴬi (or iIᴬ).\nChild with blood group O⁺ has genotype ii.\nSo only A is correct."}
